1. INTRODUCTION

.ti 3
UNIX, written at Bell Laboratories, is an operating system
for use on the PDP 11/40, 11/45 and 11/70 computers.
.ti 3
Historically, the Dept. of Computing Science became interested
in UNIX for a number of reasons.

.in 7
.ti 3
1) It provided a more flexible base system
than other proprietary software available on which to build other
systems.
.ti 3
2) It is a true time-sharing task swapping system
designed to support a number of interactive terminals.
.ti 3
3) It possessed an interesting array of software:
.in 5
	* text manipulation and type-setting programs
	* A host of languages
	* compiler compilers
	* a BCPL-like system language producing efficient code
	* an extremely fast assembler
	* a fascinating array of games including chess
	* graphics programs
.ti 3
4) It made possible the use of a large amount of software developed
elsewhere under UNIX, notably from universities in the U.S.A.
where UNIX occupies an increasingly prominent position.
.ti 3
5) It is a good vehicle for teaching a course on operating
systems.
.ti 3
6) Expansion to accomodate many peripheral device types (notably
graphics devices) is a simple procedure.
.in 0
.ti 3
Within the School of Electrical Engineering, the batch station
PDP 11/40 provided a machine to run UNIX
so long as its functioning as a batch station was not hindered.
.ti 3
Towards this end some members of the Dept. of Computing
Science wrote a batch system to run under UNIX.
To them the batch systems then in use at other batch stations
left much to be desired, requiring
an operator to be present constantly and 
having a generally poor level of automation.
A higher level of operatorless automation should
have been possible on a PDP 11/40 and far more efficient use
of the available CYBER line could be made using disc based
input and output spooling.
.ti 3
The result of their work was the first UNIX Batch System
which had a number of features not present in other systems
at the time it was written.
	* Faster card reading speeds due to spooling of card decks
	* Faster printing from the print spool area
	* More efficient printing method (less shuffles more skips)
	* Fewer meaningless messages on the console
	* Maintenance of a complete set of batch station statistics
	* Availability of information on CYBER line errors
	* No operator required for normal running
	* Elimination of operator prompting commands (done by software)

.ti 3
This system has now been running at various batch stations at UNSW since
June 1975 and
has proved to be a stable
and efficient system.
.ti 3
Early in 1976 a new release of UNIX was obtained from Bell Labs.
Level 6 was needless to say full of "new you beaut features" and,
as with Level 5, the people in the Dept of Computing Science
could not restrain their enthusiasm.
.ti 3
However enthusiasm was now tempered with experience and hindsight.
with a clearer idea of the most "fantasmagorical" way to do things
coding began on a new UNIX Batch System.
.ti 3
Chief GURU officiating at the birth of Level 6
at UNSW is Ian Johnstone (late of AAEC, now of AGSM, know as "thong")
and the bulk of the credit for additions and alterations must go to him,
although guidance, often physical, was donated by
many other enthusiasts to produce the system now being distributed to you.
.ti 3
No system is complete without adequate documentation and this manual
tries to go some of the way towards this completion.
.ti 3
UNIX provided the text editing and manipulation programs
used to prepare the manual in particular the "ROFF" program
to run off a formatted, justified, headed and paginated manual from
text entered in any fashion using simple embedded commands.
.ti 3
We believe Level 6 will prove easier to use, faster from the users point of view
and a generally more friendly system.
.ti 3
We can only hope that this manual is all
it was intended to be and hope it is of use to you.







.ti 70
THE GURUs-VI  (DCS)




P.S.

.ti 3
The Batch system has now been taken over by the Computing Services
Unit (CSU) and is fully supported by them.
Many bugs and deficiencies in the original system were corrected, and extensions
added, mostly by Dave Horsfall.
These extensions are described in appendix B.

.ti 70
DAVE H. (CSU)

